-- title:
--   Prediction limits from a completed ledger
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Fix a candidate ledger at level $k$, base points $b_j$ and an ultrafilter $\mathcal U$.
--
--   1. **Prediction limit.** For a node $v$ of level $k+1$, the weak limit along $\mathcal U$ of the predictions at the actual parameter of $v$.
--   2. **As a limit of cutoffs.** It is the weak limit of the evaluated ideal outputs:
--   $$
--   \mathrm{ev}_{k+1}(H_v^{b_j})\rightharpoonup\Lambda_v\quad\text{along }\mathcal U ,
--   $$
--   where $H_v^{b}$ keeps the retained labels left of $v$ and puts $b$ elsewhere.
--   3. **Ideal cutoff.** When the retained labels are the outputs $f_{k+1,w}(x)$, the ideal output is the ideal cutoff of the switching argument.
